Bridges Burned	1917	{"Olga Petrova","Mahlon Hamilton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Bridges Burned is a lost 1917 silent film drama directed by Perry N. Vekroff and starring Olga Petrova. Popular Plays and Players produced while Metro Pictures distributed.
Bringing Home Father	1917	{"Franklyn Farnum","Agnes Vernon"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Bringing Home Father is a 1917 American silent comedy film directed by William Worthington and starring Franklyn Farnum, Agnes Vernon and Florence Mayon.
Broadway Arizona	1917	{"Olive Thomas","George Chesebro","George Hernandez"}	{"Drama","Silent","Western"}	Broadway Arizona is a 1917 American silent Western film directed by Lynn Reynolds and starring Olive Thomas, George Chesebro and George Hernandez.
Broadway Jones	1917	{"George M. Cohan","Marguerite Snow","Crauford Kent"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Broadway Jones is a lost 1917 American silent comedy film directed by Joseph Kaufman and starring George M. Cohan, in his film debut, in a motion picture based on his 1912 play, Broadway Jones.
The Broadway Sport	1917	{"Stuart Holmes","Wanda Hawley"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Broadway Sport is a 1917 American silent comedy film directed by Carl Harbaugh and starring Stuart Holmes, Wanda Hawley, Dan Mason, Mabel Rutter, and William B. Green. The film was released by Fox Film Corporation on June 10, 1917.
The Bronze Bride	1917	{"Claire McDowell","Frank Mayo"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Bronze Bride is a 1917 American silent drama film directed by Henry MacRae and starring Claire McDowell, Frank Mayo, and Edward Clark.
Bucking Broadway	1917	{"Harry Carey","Molly Malone"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Bucking Broadway is a 1917 American silent Western film directed by John Ford, probably his sixth feature film. Long thought to be lost, along with about 60 of Ford's 70 silent films, it was found in 2002 in the archives of the CNC. It was subsequently restored and digitized and is available on the Criterion Blu-Ray of John Ford's Stagecoach.
Builders of Castles	1917	{"Marc McDermott","Miriam Nesbitt"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Builders of Castles is a 1917 silent film drama directed by Ben Turbett. It starred Marc McDermott and Miriam Nesbitt, a real-life husband and wife team. It was produced by the Edison Manufacturing Company.
The Burglar	1917	{"Carlyle Blackwell","Madge Evans","Evelyn Greeley"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Burglar is a 1917 American silent drama film directed by Harley Knoles and starring Carlyle Blackwell, Madge Evans and Evelyn Greeley.
Burning the Candle	1917	{"Henry B. Walthall","Mary Charleson"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Burning the Candle is a 1917 American silent drama film directed by Harry Beaumont and starring Henry B. Walthall, Mary Charleson and Frances Raymond.
By Right of Possession	1917	{"Antonio Moreno","Otto Lederer"}	{"Western","Silent"}	By Right of Possession is a 1917 American silent Western film directed by William Wolbert and starring Mary Anderson, Antonio Moreno and Otto Lederer.
The Call of Her People	1917	{"Ethel Barrymore","William B. Davidson","Frank Montgomery"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Call of Her People is a 1917 silent film spectacle directed by John W. Noble and starring Ethel Barrymore. It was produced and distributed by Metro Pictures. The story is based on a play by Edward Sheldon.
The Call of the East	1917	{"Sessue Hayakawa","Tsuru Aoki","Jack Holt"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Call of the East is a 1917 American silent drama film directed by George Melford and written by Beulah Marie Dix. The film stars Sessue Hayakawa, Tsuru Aoki, Jack Holt, Margaret Loomis, James Cruze, and Ernest Joy. The film was released on October 15, 1917, by Paramount Pictures. It is not known whether the film currently survives, which suggests that it is a lost film.
Camille	1917	{"Theda Bara","Alan Roscoe","Claire Whitney"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Camille is a 1917 American silent film based on the play adaptation of La Dame aux Camélias by Alexandre Dumas, fils, first published in French as a novel in 1848 and as a play in 1852. Adapted for the screen by Adrian Johnson, Camille was directed by J. Gordon Edwards and starred Theda Bara as Camille and Albert Roscoe as her lover, Armand.
The Candy Girl	1917	{"Gladys Hulette","J. H. Gilmour"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Candy Girl is a 1917 American silent drama film directed by Eugene Moore and starring Gladys Hulette, William Parke Jr., and J.H. Gilmour.
