Dead Men Tell No Tales	1920	{"Catherine Calvert","Percy Marmont","Holmes Herbert"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	Dead Men Tell No Tales is a 1920 American silent adventure film directed by Tom Terriss and starring Catherine Calvert. It was produced by Terriss and the Vitagraph Company of America with distribution by Vitagraph.
The Deadlier Sex	1920	{"Blanche Sweet","Winter Hall"}	{"Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	The Deadlier Sex is a 1920 American silent drama-comedy film directed by Robert Thornby which stars Blanche Sweet and features Boris Karloff, and was distributed by Pathé Exchange. The screenplay was written by Fred Myton, based on a short story by Bayard Veiller. It was filmed in Truckee, California. This film was recently restored by the Academy Film Archive and still exists.
Deadline at Eleven	1920	{"Corinne Griffith","Webster Campbell"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Deadline at Eleven is a lost 1920 American silent drama film directed by George Fawcett and starring Corinne Griffith. It was produced by and distributed by the Vitagraph Company of America.
The Deep Purple	1920	{"Miriam Cooper","Helen Ware"}	{"Crime","Drama","Silent"}	The Deep Purple is a 1920 American silent crime drama film directed by Raoul Walsh from a 1910 play co-written by Wilson Mizner and Paul Armstrong. The picture stars Miriam Cooper and Helen Ware and is a remake of the 1915 lost film The Deep Purple. It is not known whether the 1920 film currently survives.
Deep Waters	1920	{"Barbara Bedford","John Gilbert"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Deep Waters is a lost 1920 American silent drama film directed by Maurice Tourneur and written by F. Hopkinson Smith, Michael Morton and John Gilbert. The film stars Rudolph Christians, Barbara Bedford, John Gilbert, Florence Deshon, Jack McDonald, Henry Woodward, and George Nichols. The film was released on October 10, 1920, by Paramount Pictures.
The Desperate Hero	1920	{"Owen Moore","Gloria Hope"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Desperate Hero is a 1920 American silent comedy film directed by Wesley Ruggles and starring Owen Moore, Gloria Hope, Emmett King.
The Devil's Claim	1920	{"Sessue Hayakawa","Colleen Moore","Rhea Mitchell"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Devil's Claim is a 1920 American silent drama film starring Sessue Hayakawa and Colleen Moore. A print of this film survives.
The Devil's Garden	1920	{"Lionel Barrymore","May McAvoy","Doris Rankin"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Devil's Garden is a lost 1920 American silent drama film produced by Whitman Bennett, directed by Kenneth Webb, and released through First National Exhibitor's Circuit, which was later known as First National Pictures. The film starred Lionel Barrymore, May McAvoy, and Barrymore's first wife Doris Rankin. It is based on the 1913 novel, The Devil's Garden by William Babington Maxwell, and was the first film for Whitman Bennett Productions.
The Devil's Pass Key	1920	{"Sam de Grasse","Mae Busch"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Devil's Pass Key is a 1920 silent drama film directed by Erich von Stroheim. Considered a “lost film”, no print is officially known to exist.
The Devil to Pay	1920	{"Roy Stewart","Robert McKim","Fritzi Brunette"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	The Devil to Pay is a 1920 American silent mystery film directed by Ernest C. Warde and starring Roy Stewart, Robert McKim and Fritzi Brunette.
Dice of Destiny	1920	{"H. B. Warner","Lillian Rich"}	{"Crime","Drama","Silent"}	Dice of Destiny is a 1920 American silent crime drama film directed by Henry King and starring H. B. Warner, Lillian Rich, and Howard Davies.
Dinty	1920	{"Wesley Barry","Noah Beery","Colleen Moore"}	{"Adventure","Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	Dinty is a 1920 American silent comedy drama film written by Marshall Neilan and John McDermott specifically for Wesley Barry, a young actor known for his freckled complexion. Prominent among the supporting players were Colleen Moore, Marjorie Daw, Pat O'Malley, and Noah Beery.
