A Sailor-Made Man	1921	{"Harold Lloyd","Mildred Davis","Noah Young"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	A Sailor-Made Man is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Fred Newmeyer and starring Harold Lloyd.
Salvage	1921	{"Pauline Frederick","Ralph Lewis","Milton Sills"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Salvage is a lost 1921 American silent drama film directed by Henry King and starring Pauline Frederick. It was produced and distributed by the Robertson-Cole Company.
Salvation Nell	1921	{"Pauline Starke","Joe King","Gypsy O'Brien"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Salvation Nell is a 1921 American silent drama film produced by Whitman Bennett and distributed by Associated First National Pictures, later First National Pictures. It was directed by Kenneth Webb and stars Pauline Starke. The film is based on a successful 1908 Broadway play by Edward Sheldon that starred Minnie Maddern Fiske.
The Scarab Ring	1921	{"Alice Joyce","Joe King","Eddie Phillips"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	The Scarab Ring is a 1921 American silent mystery film directed by Edward José and starring Alice Joyce, Maude Malcolm and Joe King.
School Days	1921	{"Wesley Barry","George Lessey","Nellie Parker Spaulding"}	{"Comedy"}	School Days is a 1921 American comedy film directed by William Nigh, written by Walter DeLeon, Hoey Lawlor and William Nigh, and starring Wesley Barry, George Lessey, Nellie Parker Spaulding, Margaret Seddon, Arline Blackburn, and J.H. Gilmour. It was released by Warner Bros. on December 25, 1921 and was Warner's biggest grossing film until The Sea Beast in 1926.
Scrambled Wives	1921	{"Marguerite Clark","Pierre Gendron","Virginia Lee"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Scrambled Wives is a lost 1921 American silent comedy film produced by and starring Marguerite Clark. It was directed by Edward H. Griffith and released through Associated First National. This film had one color sequence, presumably a 1-strip Technicolor process being that Technicolor's Ray June is one of the cameramen. This film marks Clark's final screen performance. It is not known whether the film currently survives. \nThis film is based on the play The First Mrs. Chiverick by Adelaide Matthews.
Scrap Iron	1921	{"Charles Ray","Lydia Knott","Vera Steadman"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Scrap Iron is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Charles Ray and starring Ray, Lydia Knott and Vera Steadman.
The Sea Lion	1921	{"Hobart Bosworth","Bessie Love","Emory Johnson"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	The Sea Lion is a 1921 American silent adventure film directed by Rowland V. Lee, and starring Hobart Bosworth, Bessie Love, and Emory Johnson. It was produced and distributed by Associated Producers Incorporated. The team who worked on this film had previously made Lee's Blind Hearts (1921).
The Secret of the Hills	1921	{"Antonio Moreno","Kingsley Benedict","Elita Proctor Otis"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	The Secret of the Hills is a 1921 American silent mystery film directed by Chester Bennett and starring Antonio Moreno, Lillian Hall and Kingsley Benedict
See My Lawyer	1921	{"T. Roy Barnes","Grace Darmond","Lloyd Whitlock"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	See My Lawyer is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Al Christie and starring T. Roy Barnes, Grace Darmond and Lloyd Whitlock. It is based on the 1915 play See My Lawyer by Max Marcin.
Sentimental Tommy	1921	{"Gareth Hughes","George Fawcett","May McAvoy"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Sentimental Tommy is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by John S. Robertson. It featured Mary Astor in one of her earliest roles, although her scenes were deleted before release. The story is based on James M. Barrie's novel. The film, which made a star of Gareth Hughes, is now considered a lost film.
The Servant in the House	1921	{"Jean Hersholt","Jack Curtis","Claire Anderson"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Servant in the House is a lost 1921 American silent drama film directed by Jack Conway based on a 1908 Broadway play, The Servant in the House by Charles Rann Kennedy.
Seven Years Bad Luck	1921	{"Max Linder","Alta Allen","Ralph McCullough"}	{"Comedy"}	Seven Years Bad Luck is a 1921 American comedy film written and directed by, and starring Max Linder. A man about to be married becomes fearful of bad luck when he breaks a mirror.
Shadows of Conscience	1921	{"Russell Simpson","Barbara Tennant","Gertrude Olmstead"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Shadows of Conscience is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by John P. McCarthy and starring Russell Simpson, Barbara Tennant and Gertrude Olmstead.
Sham	1921	{"Ethel Clayton","Theodore Roberts","Sylvia Ashton"}	{"Romance","Drama","Silent"}	Sham is a lost 1921 American silent romantic drama directed by Thomas N. Heffron and starring Ethel Clayton and Theodore Roberts. The film is based on the 1905 play of the same name written by Elmer Harris and Geraldine Bonner, and was adapted for the screen by Douglas Z. Doty.
Shame	1921	{"John Gilbert","Anna May Wong","Doris Pawn"}	{"Drama"}	Shame is a 1921 American film directed by Emmett J. Flynn. It is based on the story Clung by Max Brand, which appeared in the magazine All Story Weekly. This black and white silent film was distributed and produced by Fox Film Corporation. It is considered a drama and has a runtime of 90 mins. It is presumed to be a lost film.
The Shark Master	1921	{"Frank Mayo","Doris Deane","Herbert Fortier"}	{"Drama"}	The Shark Master is a 1921 American drama film directed by Fred LeRoy Granville and written by George C. Hull and Fred LeRoy Granville. The film stars Frank Mayo, Doris Deane, Herbert Fortier, Oliver Cross, May Collins, and Bowditch M. Turner. The film was released on August 28, 1921, by Universal Film Manufacturing Company.
The Sheik	1921	{"Rudolph Valentino","Rosemary Theby","Agnes Ayres"}	{"Drama","Adventure","Silent","Romance"}	The Sheik is a 1921 American silent romantic drama film produced by Famous Players-Lasky, directed by George Melford, starring Rudolph Valentino and Agnes Ayres, and featuring Adolphe Menjou. It was based on the bestselling 1919 romance novel of the same name by Edith Maude Hull and was adapted for the screen by Monte M. Katterjohn. The film was a box-office hit and helped propel Valentino to stardom.
Sheltered Daughters	1921	{"Justine Johnstone","Riley Hatch","Warner Baxter"}	{"Comedy","Silent","War"}	Sheltered Daughters is a 1921 American silent film directed by Edward Dillon, starring Justine Johnstone, Riley Hatch, Charles K. Gerrard and Warner Baxter.
The Sheriff of Hope Eternal	1921	{"Jack Hoxie","Marin Sais","Joseph W. Girard"}	{"Western","Silent"}	The Sheriff of Hope Eternal is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by Ben F. Wilson and starring Jack Hoxie, Marin Sais and Joseph W. Girard.
A Shocking Night	1921	{"Eddie Lyons","Lee Moran","Alta Allen"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	A Shocking Night is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by and starring Eddie Lyons and Lee Moran. The cast also included Alta Allen and Lionel Belmore.
Short Skirts	1921	{"Gladys Walton","Ena Gregory","Jack Mower"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Short Skirts is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Harry B. Harris and starring Gladys Walton, Ena Gregory and Jack Mower.
The Sign on the Door	1921	{"Norma Talmadge","Charles Richman","Lew Cody"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Sign on the Door is a 1921 American silent drama film starring Norma Talmadge and Lew Cody. The film was directed and written by Herbert Brenon, and based upon the 1919 play of the same name by Channing Pollock. A copy of this film is in the Library of Congress film archive.
The Silent Call	1921	{"John Bowers","Kathryn McGuire","William Dyer"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	The Silent Call is a 1921 American silent adventure film directed by Laurence Trimble and featuring John Bowers, Kathryn McGuire and William Dyer. It was produced as a vehicle for the canine star Strongheart who appeared in several silent films.
Silent Years	1921	{"Rose Dione","Tully Marshall","George A. McDaniel"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Silent Years is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Louis J. Gasnier and starring Rose Dione, Tully Marshall and George A. McDaniel.
The Silver Car	1921	{"Earle Williams","Kathryn Adams","Eric Mayne"}	{"Crime","Silent"}	The Silver Car is a lost 1921 silent film crime film directed by David Smith starring Earle Williams and Kathryn Adams. It was produced and distributed by the Vitagraph Company.
The Silver Lining	1921	{"Coit Albertson","Jewel Carmen","Leslie Austin"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Silver Lining is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Roland West. It is not known whether the film currently survives.
The Sin of Martha Queed	1921	{"Mary Thurman","Joseph J. Dowling","Eugenie Besserer"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Sin of Martha Queed is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Allan Dwan and starring Mary Thurman, Joseph J. Dowling and Eugenie Besserer.
Singing River	1921	{"William Russell","Vola Vale","Clark Comstock"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Singing River is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by Charles Giblyn and starring William Russell, Vola Vale and Clark Comstock.
The Single Track	1921	{"Corinne Griffith","Richard Travers","Charles Kent"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Single Track is a lost 1921 American silent melodrama film directed by Webster Campbell and starring Corinne Griffith. The film is based upon a story by Isabelle Ostrander writing under the pseudonym Douglas Grant. The film was produced and distributed by Vitagraph.
Skirts	1921	{"Clyde Cook","Chester Conklin","Polly Moran"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Skirts is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Hampton Del Ruth and starring Clyde Cook, Chester Conklin, Polly Moran, Jack Cooper, Billy Armstrong, and Ethel Teare. The film was released by Fox Film Corporation on April 10, 1921.
The Sky Pilot	1921	{"John Bowers","Colleen Moore","David Butler"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Sky Pilot is a 1921 American silent drama film based on the novel of the same name by Ralph Connor. It is directed by King Vidor and features Colleen Moore. In February 2020, the film was shown in a newly restored version at the 70th Berlin International Film Festival, as part of a retrospective dedicated to King Vidor's career.
A Small Town Idol	1921	{"Ben Turpin","James Finlayson","Phyllis Haver"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	A Small Town Idol is a 1921 American silent feature comedy film produced by Mack Sennett and released through Associated First National. The film stars Ben Turpin and was made and acted by many of the same Sennett personnel from his previous year's Down on the Farm. Sennett and Erle C. Kenton directed.
The Smart Sex	1921	{"Eva Novak","Margaret Mann"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Smart Sex is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Fred LeRoy Granville and starring Eva Novak, Frank Braidwood and Margaret Mann.
The Snob	1921	{"Wanda Hawley","Walter Hiers","Sylvia Ashton"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Snob is a lost 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Sam Wood, written by Alice Eyton, and starring Wanda Hawley, Edwin Stevens, Walter Hiers, Sylvia Ashton, W. E. Lawrence, and Julia Faye. It was released in January 1921, by Realart Pictures Corporation.
Society Secrets	1921	{"Eva Novak","Gertrude Claire","Clarissa Selwynne"}	{"Comedy","Satire","Silent"}	Society Secrets is a 1921 American silent satire film, directed by Leo McCarey. It stars Eva Novak, Gertrude Claire, and George Verrell, and was released in February 1921. It marked McCarey's directorial debut. He didn't make a further feature film for eight years as he concentrated on writing and directing shorts.
Society Snobs	1921	{"Conway Tearle","Ida Darling","Huntley Gordon"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Society Snobs is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Hobart Henley and starring Conway Tearle, Vivian Forrester and Ida Darling.
The Son of Wallingford	1921	{"Wilfrid North","Tom Gallery","Antrim Short"}	{"Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	The Son of Wallingford is a 1921 American silent comedy drama film directed by George Randolph Chester and Lillian Josephine Chester and starring Wilfrid North, Tom Gallery and Antrim Short. It is based on George Chester's novel The Son of Wallingford about a confidence trickster, itself inspired by his Cosmopolitan articles and an earlier hit play Get-Rich-Quick Wallingford. It was shot at Vitagraph's Flatbush Studios in Brooklyn. It was released by Vitagraph a couple of months before a Paramount Pictures version of Get-Rich-Quick Wallingford.
Sowing the Wind	1921	{"Anita Stewart","James Morrison","Myrtle Stedman"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Sowing the Wind is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by John M. Stahl and starring Anita Stewart, James Morrison, and Myrtle Stedman. It is an adaptation of the British play Sowing the Wind by Sydney Grundy which had previously been turned into a 1916 film of the same title.
The Speed Girl	1921	{"Bebe Daniels","Theodore von Eltz","Frank Elliott"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Speed Girl is a lost 1921 American silent comedy film produced by Realart Pictures and released through Paramount Pictures. It was directed by Maurice Campbell, a Broadway director and producer, and starred Bebe Daniels, then a popular 20-year-old veteran film actress.
The Spenders	1921	{"Claire Adams","Robert McKim","Joseph J. Dowling"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Spenders is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Jack Conway and starring Claire Adams, Robert McKim and Joseph J. Dowling.
Steelheart	1921	{"William Duncan","Edith Johnson","Jack Curtis"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Steelheart is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by William Duncan and starring Duncan, Edith Johnson and Jack Curtis.
The Sting of the Lash	1921	{"Pauline Frederick","Clyde Fillmore","Lawson Butt"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Sting of the Lash is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Henry King and starring Pauline Frederick, Clyde Fillmore, and Lawson Butt.
Straight from Paris	1921	{"Clara Kimball Young","Bertram Grassby","Betty Francisco"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Straight from Paris is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Harry Garson and starring Clara Kimball Young, Bertram Grassby and Betty Francisco.
Straight from the Shoulder	1921	{"Buck Jones","Helen Ferguson","Kid McCoy"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Straight from the Shoulder is a lost 1921 American silent Western film directed by Bernard J. Durning and starring Buck Jones. It was produced and distributed by Fox Film Corporation.
Straight Is the Way	1921	{"Matt Moore","Mabel Bert","Gladys Leslie"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Straight Is the Way is a surviving 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Robert G. Vignola, written by Frances Marion and Ethel Watts Mumford, and starring Matt Moore, Mabel Bert, Gladys Leslie, George Parsons, Henry Sedley, Van Dyke Brooke, and Emily Fitzroy. It was released on March 6, 1921, by Paramount Pictures.
Such a Little Queen	1921	{"Constance Binney","Vincent Coleman","Frank Losee"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Such a Little Queen is a 1921 American silent film drama starring Constance Binney and directed by George Fawcett, who usually appeared in front of the camera as a character actor. This film is a remake of the 1914 film of the same name which served as an early feature length vehicle for Mary Pickford who had recently arrived at Adolph Zukor's Famous Players studio. The source material for both films was the 1909 Broadway play by Channing Pollock that starred Elsie Ferguson in a breakout stage role. It is not known whether the 1921 film currently survives.
Sunset Jones	1921	{"Charles Clary","James Gordon","Irene Rich"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Sunset Jones is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by George L. Cox and starring Charles Clary, James Gordon, and Irene Rich.
Sure Fire	1921	{"Hoot Gibson","Molly Malone","Fritzi Brunette"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Sure Fire is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by John Ford and featuring Hoot Gibson. It is considered to be a lost film.
The Swamp	1921	{"Sessue Hayakawa","Bessie Love","Lillian Langdon"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Swamp is a 1921 American silent drama film released by the Robertson-Cole Pictures Corporation and directed by Colin Campbell. The film was written and produced by Sessue Hayakawa, who also co-stars with Bessie Love. A print of this film is preserved at the Gosfilmofond archive in Moscow.
A Tale of Two Worlds	1921	{"Leatrice Joy","Wallace Beery","E. Alyn Warren"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	A Tale of Two Worlds is a 1921 American silent drama film produced and distributed by Goldwyn Pictures and directed by Frank Lloyd. The film stars several well-known actors including Leatrice Joy, Wallace Beery, Edythe Chapman, and J. Frank Glendon. The film has been preserved at the Library of Congress.
Tangled Trails	1921	{"Neal Hart","Jules Cowles","Edward Roseman"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Tangled Trails is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by Charles Bartlett and starring Neal Hart and Violet Palmer. The film is also known as Sands of Sacrifice.
The Ten Dollar Raise	1921	{"William V. Mong","Marguerite De La Motte"}	{"Comedy","Silent","War"}	The Ten Dollar Raise is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Edward Sloman and starring William V. Mong, Marguerite De La Motte, and Pat O'Malley.
Ten Nights in a Bar Room	1921	{"John Lowell","Kempton Greene"}	{"Drama"}	Ten Nights in a Bar Room is a 1921 melodrama film directed by Oscar Apfel. The film is based on the novel of the same name by Timothy Shay Arthur.
That Girl Montana	1921	{"Blanche Sweet","Mahlon Hamilton","Frank Lanning"}	{"Western","Silent"}	That Girl Montana is an extant 1921 American silent Western film starring Blanche Sweet and distributed by Pathé Exchange. Jesse D. Hampton produced and Robert Thornby directed. The film is based on a 1901 novel, That Girl Montana, by Marah Ellis Ryan. This is one of Sweet's few 1920s silent films to survive and is available in the DVD format.
Their Mutual Child	1921	{"Margarita Fischer","Nigel Barrie","Margaret Campbell"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	Their Mutual Child is a lost 1920 American silent comedy film directed by George L. Cox and starring Margarita Fischer, Joseph Bennett and Margaret Campbell. It was based on the 1919 novel of the same title by P. G. Wodehouse.
There Are No Villains	1921	{"Viola Dana","Gaston Glass","DeWitt Jennings"}	{"Crime","Drama","Silent"}	There Are No Villains is a lost 1921 American silent crime melodrama film starring Viola Dana and produced and directed by Bayard Veiller.
They Shall Pay	1921	{"Lottie Pickford","Allan Forrest","Paul Weigel"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	They Shall Pay is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Martin Justice and starring Lottie Pickford, Allan Forrest, and Paul Weigel.
The Three Musketeers	1921	{"Douglas Fairbanks","Leon Bary","Eugene Pallette"}	{"Silent"}	The Three Musketeers is a 1921 American silent film based on the 1844 novel The Three Musketeers by Alexandre Dumas, père. It was directed by Fred Niblo and stars Douglas Fairbanks as d'Artagnan. The film originally had scenes filmed in the Handschiegl Color Process. The film had a sequel, The Iron Mask (1929), also starring Fairbanks as d'Artagnan and DeBrulier as Cardinal Richelieu.
Three Sevens	1921	{"Antonio Moreno","Jean Calhoun","Emmett King"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Three Sevens is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Chester Bennett and starring Antonio Moreno, Jean Calhoun and Emmett King.
Three Word Brand	1921	{"William S. Hart","Jane Novak","Herschel Mayall"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Three Word Brand is a 1921 American silent Western film distributed by Paramount Pictures that was directed by Lambert Hillyer and starred William S. Hart and Jane Novak. Hart plays three roles in the film.
Through the Back Door	1921	{"Mary Pickford","Gertrude Astor","Wilfred Lucas"}	{"Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	Through the Back Door is a 1921 American silent comedy drama film directed by Alfred E. Green and Jack Pickford, and starring Mary Pickford.
Thunder Island	1921	{"Edith Roberts","John B. O'Brien","Arthur Jasmine"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	Thunder Island is a 1921 American silent adventure film directed by Norman Dawn and starring Edith Roberts, John B. O'Brien and Arthur Jasmine.
Tiger True	1921	{"Frank Mayo","Fritzi Brunette","Elinor Hancock"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	Tiger True is a 1921 American silent mystery film directed by J.P. McGowan and starring Frank Mayo, Fritzi Brunette and Elinor Hancock.
Tol'able David	1921	{"Richard Barthelmess","Gladys Hulette","Ernest Torrence"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Tol'able David is a 1921 American silent film based on the 1917 Joseph Hergesheimer short story of the same name. It was adapted to the screen by Edmund Goulding and directed by Henry King for Inspiration Pictures. A rustic tale of violence set in the Allegheny Mountains of eastern West Virginia, it was filmed in Blue Grass, Virginia, with some locals featured in minor roles.
Too Much Speed	1921	{"Wallace Reid","Agnes Ayres","Theodore Roberts"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Too Much Speed is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Frank Urson, written by Byron Morgan, and starring Wallace Reid, Agnes Ayres, Theodore Roberts, Jack Richardson, Lucien Littlefield, and Guy Oliver. It was released on June 5, 1921, by Paramount Pictures. It is not known whether the film currently survives.
Too Wise Wives	1921	{"Louis Calhern","Claire Windsor","Phillips Smalley"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Too Wise Wives is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Lois Weber, written by Lois Weber and Marion Orth, and starring Louis Calhern, Claire Windsor, Phillips Smalley, and Mona Lisa. It was released on May 22, 1921, by Paramount Pictures. A copy of the film is in the Library of Congress.
The Torrent	1921	{"Eva Novak","Elita Proctor Otis","Jack Perrin"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	The Torrent is a 1921 American silent adventure film directed by Stuart Paton and starring Eva Novak, Elita Proctor Otis and Jack Perrin.
Trailin'	1921	{"Tom Mix","Eva Novak","Bert Sprotte"}	{"Western","Mystery","Silent"}	Trailin' is a 1921 American silent Western mystery film directed by Lynn Reynolds and starring Tom Mix, Eva Novak and Bert Sprotte.
The Traveling Salesman	1921	{"Fatty Arbuckle","Betty Ross Clarke","Wilton Taylor"}	{"Comedy"}	The Traveling Salesman is a 1921 American comedy film starring Fatty Arbuckle. It is based on a 1908 play, The Traveling Salesman, by James Grant Forbes. A 1916 film adaptation of the play starred Frank McIntyre, who had also starred in the play. A print of The Traveling Salesman with German intertitles survives at the George Eastman House.
A Trip to Paradise	1921	{"Bert Lytell","Virginia Valli","Brinsley Shaw"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	A Trip to Paradise is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Maxwell Karger and starring Bert Lytell, Virginia Valli, and Brinsley Shaw. It was released on September 5, 1921.
Tropical Love	1921	{"Ruth Clifford","Reginald Denny","Huntley Gordon"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Tropical Love is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Ralph Ince and starring Ruth Clifford, Reginald Denny and Huntley Gordon. It was partly shot on location in Puerto Rico.
The Truant Husband	1921	{"Mahlon Hamilton","Betty Blythe","Francelia Billington"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Truant Husband is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Thomas N. Heffron and starring Mahlon Hamilton, Betty Blythe and Francelia Billington.
Two Minutes to Go	1921	{"Charles Ray","Mary Anderson","Lionel Belmore"}	{"Sports","Comedy","Drama","Silent","Sport"}	Two Minutes to Go is a 1921 American silent sport comedy-drama film directed by Charles Ray and written by Richard Andres. The film stars Charles Ray, Mary Anderson, Lionel Belmore, Lincoln Stedman, Truman Van Dyke, and Gus Leonard. The film was released by Associated First National on October 17, 1921.
Two Weeks with Pay	1921	{"Bebe Daniels","Jack Mulhall","George Periolat"}	{"Comedy","Romance","Silent"}	Two Weeks with Pay is a lost 1921 American silent comedy romance film starring Bebe Daniels and directed by Maurice Campbell.
Uncharted Seas	1921	{"Alice Lake","Carl Gerard","Rudolph Valentino"}	{"Romance","Drama","Silent"}	Uncharted Seas is a 1921 American silent romance drama film directed by Wesley Ruggles and starring Alice Lake, Carl Gerard, and Rudolph Valentino.
Under the Lash	1921	{"Gloria Swanson","Mahlon Hamilton","Lillian Leighton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Under the Lash is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Sam Wood and starring Gloria Swanson. The film is based on the 1906 play The Shulamite by Claude Askew and Edward Knoblock, and the 1904 novel of the same name by Alice and Claude Askew. The film is lost with no copies of it existing in any archives.
The Unknown Wife	1921	{"Edith Roberts","Spottiswoode Aitken","Casson Ferguson"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Unknown Wife is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by William Worthington and starring Edith Roberts, Spottiswoode Aitken and Casson Ferguson. It is also known by the alternative title of Three at the Table.
An Unwilling Hero	1921	{"Will Rogers","Molly Malone","John Bowers"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	An Unwilling Hero is a 1921 American silent comedy film directed by Clarence G. Badger and written by Arthur F. Statter. The film stars Will Rogers, Molly Malone, John Bowers, Darrell Foss, and Jack Curtis. The film was released on May 8, 1921, by Goldwyn Pictures.
A Virgin Paradise	1921	{"Pearl White","Robert Elliott","Charles Sutton"}	{"Adventure","Silent"}	A Virgin Paradise is a lost 1921 American silent adventure film produced and distributed by Fox Film Corporation and starring serial queen Pearl White. It was directed by veteran director J. Searle Dawley.
A Virginia Courtship	1921	{"May McAvoy","Alec B. Francis","Jane Keckley"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	A Virginia Courtship is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Frank O'Connor and written by Edfrid A. Bingham based upon the play of the same name by Eugene Wiley Presbrey. The film stars May McAvoy, Alec B. Francis, Jane Keckley, L. M. Wells, Casson Ferguson, Kathlyn Williams, and Richard Tucker. The film was released in December 1921, by Paramount Pictures.
A Voice in the Dark	1921	{"Ramsey Wallace","Irene Rich","Alec B. Francis"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	A Voice in the Dark is a 1921 American black-and-white silent mystery film directed by Frank Lloyd and starring Ramsey Wallace, Irene Rich, and Alec B. Francis. The film is based on the play A Voice in the Dark by Ralph E. Dyar.
Voices of the City	1921	{"Lon Chaney","Leatrice Joy","John Bowers"}	{"Crime","Drama","Silent"}	Voices of the City is a 1921 American silent crime drama film starring Leatrice Joy and Lon Chaney that was directed by Wallace Worsley, based on the Leroy Scott novel The Night Rose. The film took more than 9 months to be released due to a controversy over the proposed title and the film's abundance of gunplay. The film was retitled Voices of the City and was only released in December 1921, although it had been completed in early March. The film is still listed under The Night Rose in some reference sources.
The Wakefield Case	1921	{"Herbert Rawlinson","Charles Dalton","Jere Austin"}	{"Mystery","Silent"}	The Wakefield Case is a 1921 American silent mystery film directed by George Irving, produced by Lois Weber and starring Herbert Rawlinson. It was released through World Film Company.
The Wallop	1921	{"Harry Carey","Charles Le Moyne","William Steele"}	{"Western","Silent"}	The Wallop is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by John Ford and starring Harry Carey. The film is considered to be lost.
The Way of a Maid	1921	{"Elaine Hammerstein","Diana Allen","Arthur Housman"}	{"Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	The Way of a Maid is a surviving 1921 American silent comedy-drama film produced by Selznick Pictures and starring Elaine Hammerstein. It was released by Select Pictures and directed by William P. S. Earle from an original story for the screen. A print of the film is held by the Library of Congress.
Wealth	1921	{"Ethel Clayton","Herbert Rawlinson","Larry Steers"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	Wealth is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by William Desmond Taylor, written by Cosmo Hamilton and Julia Crawford Ivers, and starring Ethel Clayton, Herbert Rawlinson, J.M. Dumont, Larry Steers, George Periolat, and Claire McDowell. It was released on August 21, 1921, by Paramount Pictures. It is not known whether the film currently survives, and it may be a lost film.
Wedding Bells	1921	{"Constance Talmadge","Harrison Ford","Ida Darling"}	{"Comedy","Silent","Romance"}	Wedding Bells is a lost 1921 American silent romantic-comedy film directed by Chester Withey and starring Constance Talmadge and Harrison Ford.
What Do Men Want?	1921	{"Claire Windsor","J. Frank Glendon","George Hackathorne"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What Do Men Want? is a 1921 American silent drama film written, produced, and directed by Lois Weber and starring her muse Claire Windsor. Surviving reels were released on DVD and Blu-ray in 2018.
What Every Woman Knows	1921	{"Lois Wilson","Conrad Nagel","Fred Huntley"}	{"Comedy","Drama","Silent"}	What Every Woman Knows is a 1921 American silent comedy-drama film adapted form the play What Every Woman Knows by James Barrie. The play had premiered on Broadway in 1908 and was a hit starring Maude Adams. A British silent film version had been made in 1917 and a later American talkie would be produced in 1934 with Helen Hayes. This silent film version was directed by William C. deMille continuing his forte at adapting literary and/or stage plays to the silent screen. The film stars Lois Wilson and Conrad Nagel. This version is now lost.
What Love Will Do	1921	{"Edna Murphy","Johnnie Walker","Barbara Tennant"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What Love Will Do is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by William K. Howard and starring Edna Murphy, Johnnie Walker and Barbara Tennant.
What No Man Knows	1921	{"Clara Kimball Young","Lowell Sherman","William P. Carleton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What No Man Knows is a 1921 silent film drama produced and directed by Harry Garson and starring Clara Kimball Young.
What Women Will Do	1921	{"Anna Q. Nilsson","Allan Forrest","Earl Metcalfe"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What Women Will Do is a 1921 American silent drama film directed by Edward José and starring Anna Q. Nilsson, Earl Metcalfe, and Allan Forrest.
What's a Wife Worth?	1921	{"Casson Ferguson","Ruth Renick","Cora Drew"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What's a Wife Worth? is a 1921 silent American melodrama film directed by Christy Cabanne and starring Casson Ferguson, Ruth Renick, and Cora Drew. It was released on March 27, 1921.
What's Worth While?	1921	{"Claire Windsor","Louis Calhern","Edwin Stevens"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What's Worth While? is a 1921 American silent drama film written and directed by Lois Weber and starring Claire Windsor, Arthur Stuart Hull, Mona Lisa, Louis Calhern, and Edwin Stevens. The film was released on February 27, 1921, by Paramount Pictures.
What's Your Reputation Worth?	1921	{"Corinne Griffith","Percy Marmont","Robert Gaillard"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	What's Your Reputation Worth? is a lost 1921 American silent drama film directed by Webster Campbell and starring Corinne Griffith, Percy Marmont, and Leslie Roycroft.
