The Adventures of Tarzan	1921	{"Elmo Lincoln","Louise Lorraine","Scott Pembroke"}	{}	The Adventures of Tarzan (1921) is a 15 chapter movie serial which features the third and final appearance of Elmo Lincoln as Tarzan. The serial was produced by Louis Weiss, written by Robert F. Hill and Lillian Valentine, and directed by Robert F. Hill and Scott Sidney. The first chapter was released on December 1, 1921.
The Avenging Arrow	1921	{"Ruth Roland","Edward Hearn"}	{"Silent","Western"}	The Avenging Arrow is a 1921 American silent Western film serial directed by William J. Bowman and W. S. Van Dyke. Its 15 episodes are now considered to be lost.
Double Adventure	1921	{"Charles Hutchison","Josie Sedgwick","Carl Stockdale"}	{"Adventure"}	Double Adventure is a 1921 American film serial directed by W. S. Van Dyke. The film is considered to be lost in the United States. All or parts of the serial are held by Gosfilmofond, Russian State Archive.
Among Those Present	1921	{"Harold Lloyd"}	{"Comedy"}	Among Those Present is a 1921 American "three-reeler" silent comedy film directed by Fred C. Newmeyer and starring Harold Lloyd, Mildred Davis and Mary Pickford.
Bandits Beware	1921	{"Hoot Gibson"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Bandits Beware is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by Lee Kohlmar and featuring Hoot Gibson.
Beating the Game	1921	{"Hoot Gibson"}	{"Western","Silent"}	Beating the Game is a 1921 American silent Western film directed by Lee Kohlmar and featuring Hoot Gibson.
The Boat	1921	{"Buster Keaton"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Boat is a 1921 American two-reel silent comedy film written and directed by, and starring Buster Keaton. Contemporary reviews consider it one of his best shorts, with One Week (1920), The Playhouse (1921) and Cops (1922). It is presently in the public domain. The International Buster Keaton Society takes its name, The Damfinos, from the name of the film's boat.
Brownie's Little Venus	1921	{"Baby Peggy"}	{"Short","Silent"}	\n\nBrownie's Little Venus is a 1921 American silent short film written and directed by Fred Fishback for Century Film Company and starring Baby Peggy and Brownie the Wonder Dog. It premiered at the Rivoli Theatre in New York City, New York, United States, on September 11, 1921. It was released nationwide on September 14, 1921.
The Cactus Kid	1921	{"Hoot Gibson"}	{"Western","Short","Silent"}	The Cactus Kid is a 1921 American short silent Western film directed by Lee Kohlmar and featuring Hoot Gibson.
