The Lane That Had No Turning	1922	{"Agnes Ayres","Theodore Kosloff","Mahlon Hamilton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Lane That Had No Turning is a lost 1922 American silent drama film that was directed by Victor Fleming. It was produced by Famous Players-Lasky and released through Paramount Pictures. It is based on the short novel with the same title by Gilbert Parker, which is included as the title story of Parker's 1900 collection The Lane that Had No Turning, and Other Tales Concerning the People of Pontiac.
The Lavender Bath Lady	1922	{"Gladys Walton","Edmund Burns"}	{"Comedy","Silent"}	The Lavender Bath Lady is a 1922 American silent comedy film directed by King Baggot and written by George Randolph Chester and Doris Schroeder. The film stars Gladys Walton, Charlotte Pierce, Edmund Burns, Tom Ricketts, Lydia Yeamans Titus, and Mary Winston. The film was released on November 13, 1922, by Universal Film Manufacturing Company.
The Law and the Woman	1922	{"Betty Compson","William P. Carleton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Law and the Woman is a lost 1922 American silent drama film directed by Penrhyn Stanlaws and starring Betty Compson. This film is a version of Clyde Fitch's play The Woman in the Case and a remake of a 1916 silent version The Woman in the Case starring Pauline Frederick. Jesse Lasky produced.
The Light in the Dark	1922	{"Lon Chaney","Hope Hampton"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Light in the Dark is a 1922 American silent drama film directed by Clarence Brown and stars Lon Chaney and Hope Hampton. A still exists showing Lon Chaney in the role of Tony Pantelli. Some scenes were filmed in Color.
The Lights of New York	1922	{"Estelle Taylor","Charles K. Gerrard"}	{"Drama","Silent"}	The Lights of New York is a 1922 American silent drama film directed by Charles Brabin and starring Clarence Nordstrom, Margaret Seddon and Frank Currier.
