Nobody Lives Forever	1946	{"John Garfield","Geraldine Fitzgerald"}	{"Noir","Crime"}	Nobody Lives Forever is a 1946 American crime film noir directed by Jean Negulesco and based on the novel I Wasn't Born Yesterday by W. R. Burnett. It stars John Garfield and Geraldine Fitzgerald and features Walter Brennan, Faye Emerson, George Coulouris and George Tobias.
Nocturne	1946	{"George Raft","Lynn Bari","Virginia Huston"}	{"Noir"}	Nocturne is a 1946 American film noir starring George Raft and Lynn Bari. The film was produced by longtime Alfred Hitchcock associate Joan Harrison, scripted by Jonathan Latimer, and directed by Edwin L. Marin. It was one of several medium budget thrillers Raft made in the late 1940s.
No Leave, No Love	1946	{"Van Johnson","Patricia Kirkwood"}	{"Musical"}	No Leave, No Love  is a 1946 American musical film directed by Charles Martin and starring Van Johnson, Keenan Wynn and Pat Kirkwood.
Notorious	1946	{"Cary Grant","Ingrid Bergman","Claude Rains","Louis Calhern"}	{"Suspense","Noir","Spy"}	Notorious is a 1946 American spy film noir directed and produced by Alfred Hitchcock, starring Cary Grant, Ingrid Bergman, and Claude Rains as three people whose lives become intimately entangled during an espionage operation.
The Notorious Lone Wolf	1946	{"Gerald Mohr","Janis Carter","Eric Blore"}	{"Adventure"}	The Notorious Lone Wolf (1946) is the twelfth Lone Wolf film produced by Columbia Pictures. The picture features Gerald Mohr in his inaugural performance as the protagonist detective Lone Wolf, Janis Carter, and Ian Wolfe as Adam Wainwright, the film's antagonist. The film was directed by D. Ross Lederman and written by Martin Berkeley, Edward Dein, and William J. Bowers.
Of Human Bondage	1946	{"Paul Henreid","Eleanor Parker","Janis Paige"}	{"Drama"}	Of Human Bondage is a 1946 American drama film directed by Edmund Goulding. The second screen adaptation of W. Somerset Maugham's 1915 novel, this Warner Bros. sanitized version was written by Catherine Turney. The central characters are Philip Carey, a clubfooted medical student, and Mildred Rogers, a low-class waitress with whom he becomes obsessed.
One Exciting Week	1946	{"Al Pearce","Jerome Cowan"}	{"Comedy"}	One Exciting Week is a 1946 American comedy film directed by William Beaudine and written by Jack Townley and John K. Butler. The film stars Al Pearce, Pinky Lee, Jerome Cowan, Shemp Howard, Arlene Harris and Mary Treen. It was released on June 8, 1946 by Republic Pictures.
One More Tomorrow	1946	{"Ann Sheridan","Jane Wyman"}	{"Drama"}	One More Tomorrow is a 1946 American film directed by Peter Godfrey and written by Charles Hoffman and Catherine Turney from the play The Animal Kingdom by Philip Barry. The film, starring Ann Sheridan, Dennis Morgan, Jack Carson, Alexis Smith, Jane Wyman and Reginald Gardiner, is a remake of the 1932 film The Animal Kingdom. It was released by Warner Bros. on June 1, 1946.
One Way to Love	1946	{"Marguerite Chapman","Chester Morris","Janis Carter"}	{"Comedy"}	One Way to Love is a 1946 American comedy film starring Willard Parker and Chester Morris. It was directed by Ray Enright.
O.S.S.	1946	{"Alan Ladd","Geraldine Fitzgerald","Patric Knowles"}	{"Suspense","War"}	O.S.S. is a 1946 American war film starring Alan Ladd and Geraldine Fitzgerald about the Office of Strategic Services. It was directed by Irving Pichel, who would later be blacklisted by the House Un-American Activities Committee, and written by Richard Maibaum, a World War II veteran who would later write twelve of the first 15 James Bond films. Maibaum, a former Broadway actor, also narrates the film.
Our Hearts Were Growing Up	1946	{"Gail Russell","Diana Lynn"}	{"Comedy"}	Our Hearts Were Growing Up is a 1946 American comedy film directed by William D. Russell and written by Melvin Frank, Norman Panama and Frank Waldman. It is the sequel to the 1944 film Our Hearts Were Young and Gay. The film stars Gail Russell, Diana Lynn, Brian Donlevy, Billy De Wolfe, James Brown and Bill Edwards. The film was released on June 16, 1946, by Paramount Pictures.
